      "Language": "pol",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
          "content": "The following study has been conducted to evaluate the efficacy of computer-controlled bipolar vessel sealing system (ThermoStapler, EMED), as an alternative to sutures, in order to achieve haemostasis during vaginal hysterectomy."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MATERIAL AND METHODS",
          "content": "One hundred and twenty patients have undergone vaginal hysterectomy, half of them with the use of ThermoStapler vessel sealing system (n = 60) and the other half with standard surgical technique (n = 60). Data compared in this retrospective study included the time of surgery, hemoglobin loss after the surgery, postoperative need for pain medication, duration of the hospital stay and intra/post-operative complications."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The time of surgery proved to be shorter, although not significantly, in the group of vaginal hysterectomy with sutures when compared to the other group. The intra-operative blood loss, confirmed by pre- and postoperative hemoglobin measurements, appeared to be slightly lower in the ThermoStapler group. However, postoperative need for pain medication was significantly less frequent in the bipolar forceps group (P < 0.05). Complications and the duration of the hospital stay were similar in case of both groups."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "ThermoStapler bipolar vessel sealing system (EMED) proves to be a valuable alternative to traditional technique using surgical sutures, and an effective and safe method of achieving haemostasis during vaginal hysterectomy, resulting in significantly reduced need for pain medication during hospitalization."
        }
      ]},
